{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2759 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "63b7e727573678694d85c5b627471d86617f2cf755d71fa77c63a41303a43986" :edb/db-before-t 2758 :edb/db-after-t 2759 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536071 50 "2026-10-16T00:32:50.768Z" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186071546 1078 17592186071548 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186073714 1038 17592186564473 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186073714 1038 17592186564537 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186073913 1038 17592186564489 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186073914 1038 17592186564503 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186074218 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186074218 1071 "Given that $△ABC∼△A^{′}B^{′}C^{′}$, $AB = 4cm$ and $BC = 6cm$,\nwhich of the following could be the values of $A^{′}B^{′}$ and $B^{′}C^{′}$?\n\n1. $A^{′}B^{′} = 16cm, B^{′}C^{′} = 24cm$\n2. $A^{′}B^{′} = 20cm, B^{′}C^{′} = 30cm$\n3. $A^{′}B^{′} = 32cm, B^{′}C^{′} = 42cm$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186074218 1071 "Given that $△ABC∼△{A}^{′}{B}^{′}{C}^{′},$ $AB=4\\,cm$ and $BC=6\\,cm,$\nwhich of the following could be the values of ${A}^{′}{B}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}{C}^{′}?$\n\nI. ${A}^{′}{B}^{′}=16\\,cm,\\,{B}^{′}{C}^{′}=24\\,cm$\n\nII. ${A}^{′}{B}^{′}=20\\,cm,\\,{B}^{′}{C}^{′}=30\\,cm$\n\nIII. ${A}^{′}{B}^{′}=32\\,cm,\\,{B}^{′}{C}^{′}=42\\,cm$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186074218 1074 "For similar polygons, the ratios of lengths of the corresponding sides should be the same.\n\n- Let's check for this in option I:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{AB}{{A}^{′}{B}^{′}} & =\\frac{4}{16}=\\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\frac{BC}{{B}^{′}{C}^{′}} & =\\frac{6}{24}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option I does give possible values of ${A}^{′}{B}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}{C}^{′}.$\n\n- For option II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{AB}{{A}^{′}{B}^{′}} & =\\frac{4}{20}=\\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\frac{BC}{{B}^{′}{C}^{′}} & =\\frac{6}{30}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option II does give possible values of ${A}^{′}{B}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}{C}^{′}.$\n\n- For option III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{AB}{{A}^{′}{B}^{′}} & =\\frac{4}{32}=\\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\frac{BC}{{B}^{′}{C}^{′}} & =\\frac{6}{42}=\\frac{1}{7}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option III does not give possible values of ${A}^{′}{B}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}{C}^{′}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186083331 1038 17592186564466 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186105580 1071 "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 4}\\log_2 (x+4) .$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186105580 1071 "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 4}\\log_2 (x+4)$." 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186105580 1074 "The function $\\log_2 (x+4)$ is defined for $x>-4$, so the limit as $x \\to 4$ exists.\n\nTo find this limit, we substitute $x=4$ into the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 4)\\log_{2} (x + 4) &= \\log_{2} (4 + 4) \\\\\n&= \\log_{2} (8) \\\\\n&= \\log_{2} (2^{3}) \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186105580 1074 "The function $\\log_2 (x+4)$ is defined for $x>-4,$ so the limit as $x \\to 4$ exists.\n\nTo find this limit, we substitute $x=4$ into the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}{log}_{2}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{2}⁡(4+4) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(8) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186105581 1071 "Find $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\log_2 \\left(x-1\\right)}.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186105581 1071 "Find $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\log_2 \\left(x-1\\right)}$." 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186105581 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\log_2 \\left(x-1\\right)\n$$\n\n![](images/f0/f00e550e8b543f64effcbafb43aed9e43a4363a0c3060da4fcc23d18f76a872f.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\log_2 \\left(x-1\\right) = \\infty\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186105581 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y =  \\log_2 \\left(x-1\\right).$\n\n![](images/f0/f00e550e8b543f64effcbafb43aed9e43a4363a0c3060da4fcc23d18f76a872f.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\log_2 \\left(x-1\\right) = \\infty.\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186105582 1071 "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2} \\ln (x-2)$." 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186105582 1071 "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2} \\ln (x-2).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186105582 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\ln(x-2)\n$$\n\n![](images/bf/bfe277aaf4c57d73d2649d8ade756cf04411ba23a049a6e1283d18c3e711a7fa.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for\n\n$$\nx\\leq 2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2^{-}} \\ln (x-2) = \\textrm{DNE}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x\\gt 2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2^{+}} \\ln (x-2) = -\\infty\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2} \\ln (x-2) = \\textrm{DNE}\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186105582 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y = \\ln(x-2).$\n\n![](images/bf/bfe277aaf4c57d73d2649d8ade756cf04411ba23a049a6e1283d18c3e711a7fa.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\leq 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2^{-}} \\ln (x-2) = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x\\gt 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2^{+}} \\ln (x-2) = -\\infty.\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2} \\ln (x-2) = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186105583 1071 "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{-}} \\ln (x+2)$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{+}} \\ln (x+2)$." 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186105583 1071 "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{-}} \\ln (x+2)$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{+}} \\ln (x+2).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186105583 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\ln(x+2)\n$$\n\n![](images/90/906449fdcfacb0dc4aabcd51d363a71ca4842fd1a70c4b8537d38fe1f52ef7d8.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for\n\n$$\nx\\leq -2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{-}} \\ln (x+2) = \\textrm{DNE}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x\\gt -2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{+}} \\ln (x+2) = -\\infty\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186105583 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y = \\ln(x+2).$\n\n![](images/90/906449fdcfacb0dc4aabcd51d363a71ca4842fd1a70c4b8537d38fe1f52ef7d8.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\leq -2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{-}} \\ln (x+2) = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x\\gt -2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{+}} \\ln (x+2) = -\\infty.\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186105723 1038 17592186564486 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186105723 1038 17592186564525 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186105724 1038 17592186564471 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186105724 1038 17592186564521 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186105725 1038 17592186564474 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186105725 1038 17592186564478 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186105726 1038 17592186564476 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186105726 1038 17592186564509 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186106004 1050 480.0 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186107069 1064 "Let's look at the graph of $y=\\log_2{x}$ below.\n\n![](images/00/004265d2269924c6ce8905b6b92e9050b84066b3b5a3cc5383c75dd08e62f413.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to3}\\log_2{x} = \\log_2{3}\\approx 1.58\n$$\n\nFor logarithmic functions, the limit at a point is equal to the value of the function at that point, so for any base $b \\gt 1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c}\\log_b{x} = \\log_b{c}\n$$\n\nRemember that logarithms are only defined for positive inputs $x\\gt 0$, so we must have $c>0$. Otherwise, the limit does not exist $\\textrm{(DNE})$." 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186107069 1064 "Let's look at the graph of $y=\\log_2{x}$ below.\n\n![](images/00/004265d2269924c6ce8905b6b92e9050b84066b3b5a3cc5383c75dd08e62f413.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to3}\\log_2{x} = \\log_2{3}\\approx 1.58.\n$$\n\nFor logarithmic functions, the limit at a point is equal to the value of the function at that point, so for any base $b \\gt 1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c}\\log_b{x} = \\log_b{c}.\n$$\n\nRemember that logarithms are only defined for positive inputs $x\\gt 0,$ so we must have $c>0.$ Otherwise, the limit does not exist $\\textrm{(DNE}).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186107070 1064 "Let's take a look again at the function\n\n$$\ny=\\log_2(x)\n$$\n\n![](images/30/30a66b56ea1422fc61941d22aedbbf3f17e06810e66e6590490bd4b7188fd361.png)\n\nAs $x$ gets larger and larger, the curve increases without bound. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\infty} \\log_2{x}= \\infty\n$$\n\nThe curve is not defined for\n\n$$\nx\\leq 0\n$$\n\nbut as $x$ approaches zero from the right the curve *decreases* without bound. So\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to0^+}\\log_2{x} = -\\infty\n$$\n\nAlso, note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to0^-}\\log_2{x} = \\textrm{DNE},\\qquad\\qquad \\lim_{x\\to0}\\log_2{x} = \\textrm{DNE}\n$$\n\nThese properties are true for logarithms of any base $b\\gt 1$." 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186107070 1064 "Let's take a look again at the function $y=\\log_2(x).$\n\n![](images/30/30a66b56ea1422fc61941d22aedbbf3f17e06810e66e6590490bd4b7188fd361.png)\n\nAs $x$ gets larger and larger, the curve increases without bound. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\infty} \\log_2{x}= \\infty.\n$$\n\nThe curve is not defined for $x\\leq 0,$ but as $x$ approaches zero from the right the curve *decreases* without bound. So\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to0^+}\\log_2{x} = -\\infty.\n$$\n\nAlso, note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to0^-}\\log_2{x} = \\textrm{DNE},\\qquad\\qquad \\lim_{x\\to0}\\log_2{x} = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$\n\nThese properties are true for logarithms of any base $b\\gt 1.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186110630 1038 17592186564513 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186115031 1071 "What is the range of $f(x) = 5\\log_6(x-1)-4$?" 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186115031 1071 "What is the range of $f(x) = 5\\log_6(x-1)-4?$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186115031 1074 "The range of the function\n\n$$\ny = \\log_{6}(x)\n$$\n\nis $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nFor a logarithmic function, horizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x) \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$." 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186115031 1074 "The range of the function $y = \\log_{6}(x)$ is $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$ \n\nFor a logarithmic function, horizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x) \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186115036 1071 "What is the domain of $f(x)=\\ln (2x+4)-2$?" 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186115036 1071 "What is the domain of $f(x)=\\ln (2x+4)-2?$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186115036 1074 "The domain of $y = \\ln x$ is\n\n$$\nx \\gt 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x),$ we replace $x$ with $2x+4$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n2x+4 > 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4 & >0 \\\\ 2x & >-4 \\\\ x & >-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y = f(x)$ is $x\\in\\left( -2, \\infty \\right).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186115036 1074 "The domain of $y = \\ln x$ is\n$x \\gt 0$.\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x)$, we replace $x$ with $2x+4$ in the above inequality. This gives\n$2x+4 > 0$.\n\nSolving this inequality, we obtain\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2x + 4 & > 0 \\\\ 2x & > - 4 \\\\ x & > - 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y = f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx\\in\\left(-2, \\infty \\right)\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186115037 1071 "Find the root of $f(x) = \\log(2x) - 2$." 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186115037 1071 "Find the root of $f(x) = \\log(2x) - 2.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186115037 1074 "To find the root of $f(x)$, we must solve\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\log(2x) - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log (2x) - 2 &= 0 \\\\\n\\log (2x) &= 2 \\\\\n10^{\\log (2x)} &= 10^{2} \\\\\n2x &= 100 \\\\\nx &= 50\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=50$." 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186115037 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\log(2x) - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x)-2 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(2x) & =2 \\\\ {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x)} & ={10}^{2} \\\\ 2x & =100 \\\\ x & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=50.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186115038 1071 "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x) = -\\ln(4x)$." 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186115038 1071 "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x) = -\\ln(4x).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186115038 1074 "The equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $y = \\ln{x}$ is $x=0$.\n\nTo find the equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x)$, we set the argument of the logarithmic term equal to zero, and solve for $x$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x = 0$." 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186115038 1074 "The equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $y = \\ln{x}$ is $x=0.$\n\nTo find the equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x),$ we set the argument of the logarithmic term equal to zero, and solve for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x = 0.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186115202 1038 17592186564500 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186115202 1038 17592186564523 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186115206 1038 17592186564470 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186115207 1038 17592186564475 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186115311 1038 17592186564518 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186115408 1038 17592186564496 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186115410 1038 17592186564510 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186115410 1038 17592186564516 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186115436 1050 420.0 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186116480 1064 "When we transform a logarithmic function\n\n$$\ny = \\log_a (x)\n$$\n\nits domain might be modified as well.\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows the graph of the transformed logarithmic function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\log_2(x-1)\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of $f(x)$?\n\n![](images/f1/f135e52b40df0923dea0bfd58a3255a096cab4ab23faa7c761ac5998fdfdd782.png)\n\nFirst, recall that the domain of\n\n$$\ny=\\log_a (x)\n$$\n\nis\n$x > 0$.\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x)$, we take the above inequality and replace $x$ with the argument (or input) of the logarithm function contained in $f(x)$. In this case, the argument is $x-1$, so we get\n$x-1 > 0$.\n\nSolving this inequality, we obtain\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x - 1 & > 0 \\\\ x & > 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in(1, \\infty)$." 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186116480 1064 "When we transform a logarithmic function $y = \\log_a (x),$ its domain might be modified as well.\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows the graph of the transformed logarithmic function $f(x) = \\log_2(x-1).$ What is the domain of $f(x)?$\n\n![](images/f1/f135e52b40df0923dea0bfd58a3255a096cab4ab23faa7c761ac5998fdfdd782.png)\n\nFirst, recall that the domain of $y=\\log_a (x)$ is\n\n$$\nx > 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x),$ we take the above inequality and replace $x$ with the argument (or input) of the logarithm function contained in $f(x).$ In this case, the argument is $x-1,$ so we get\n\n$$\nx-1 > 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in(1, \\infty).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186116527 1064 "When we transform a logarithmic function\n\n$$\ny = \\log_a (x)\n$$\n\nits range is not affected.\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows the graph of the transformed logarithmic function\n\n$$\nf(x) = 3\\log_2(x-1)+1\n$$\n\n![](images/4a/4a5bad1159efe184b0afc2731ebd6b5dd6149a0a1ba2d530cfdc769ab2acf250.png)\n\nThe range of $\\log_2 x$ is\n$\\log_2 x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nBecause the range of the logarithm function is the interval containing all real numbers, horizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect its range. Therefore, the range of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186116527 1064 "When we transform a logarithmic function $y = \\log_a (x),$ its range is not affected.\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows the graph of the transformed logarithmic function $f(x) = 3\\log_2(x-1)+1.$ \n\n![](images/4a/4a5bad1159efe184b0afc2731ebd6b5dd6149a0a1ba2d530cfdc769ab2acf250.png)\n\nThe range of $\\log_2 x$ is\n\n$$\n\\log_2 x 