uld be $a_{2}$ if\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186120320 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]=-4[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=-4{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{1}-4{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=4$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186121122 1064 "We say that two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly independent** if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = 0.$ Otherwise, if there exists any solution other than $x_1 = x_2 = 0,$ then we say that the two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly dependent**.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider two sets of vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/46/46c22d599752ded730a67bfd57b4dfe238e512f6dff2d561afaf2f145c8118c2.svg)\n\n- For the first set ${\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is true only if $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ is *linearly independent*.\n\n- On the other hand, for the second set ${\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has solutions other than $x_1=x_2=0.$ For example, $x_1=2$ and $x_2=1$ gives\n![](images/14/145f500e491087587478f03bd52ae180a7b4237acf17db0317ca07d28f389c56.svg)\nTherefore, the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ is *not* linearly independent. Rather, it is *linearly dependent*.\n\n**Note:** You may notice that the vectors in the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ were parallel (i.e. multiples of one another), while the vectors in set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ were not. This trend holds in general, for all sets of two vectors:\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel, while\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly independent* if and only if the two vectors are not parallel." 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186121122 1064 "We say that two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly independent** if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = 0.$ Otherwise, if there exists any solution other than $x_1 = x_2 = 0,$ then we say that the two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly dependent**.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider two sets of vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/46/46c22d599752ded730a67bfd57b4dfe238e512f6dff2d561afaf2f145c8118c2.svg)\n\n- For the first set ${\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is true only if $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ is *linearly independent*.\n\n- On the other hand, for the second set ${\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has solutions other than $x_1=x_2=0.$ For example, $x_1=2$ and $x_2=1$ gives\n![](images/14/145f500e491087587478f03bd52ae180a7b4237acf17db0317ca07d28f389c56.svg)\nTherefore, the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ is *not* linearly independent. Rather, it is *linearly dependent*.\n\n**Note:** You may notice that the vectors in the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ were parallel (i.e. multiples of one another), while the vectors in set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ were not. This trend holds in general, for all sets of two vectors:\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel, while\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly independent* if and only if the two vectors are not parallel." 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186121126 1064 "A **subspace** of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^n$ that is closed  under vector addition and scalar multiplication. In other words: \n\n$H\\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nFor example, consider the line $L$ with equation $y=3x$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2?$ \n\nFirst, notice that we can express the points on $L$ as the following set:\n\n![](images/1f/1f380f9928da2dd44ff157a72fce97c02ae44626df515d52ece6ff5feee302d2.svg)\n\nIn other words, $L$ contains all vectors of the form ![](images/90/90996c2ffae29c3bd9cd3ab398cca4dd7e1b8fbe275de8520a72a16e504fb0f3.svg) where $x$ is a real number.\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R^2,$ we need to check the two conditions above. \n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, ![](images/59/59be22e80d0be32c84a8c2bdb2f2c96b23a0941d4aba26bdefa078fd299c137c.svg) and ![](images/d7/d78ea4fcfbd469e88b7fe46e28ac6f396a538c577f4defdd1b3d60e60df3c4b7.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}+3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, ![](images/75/759c7883ad2772f20a090ad2f8c5dfe01031ca31e2b5f0e7681c945c9256c91d.svg) Then, for any scalar $c$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}c{u}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n**Note:** Since the second condition must be satisfied for any $c,$ including $c=0,$ we can conclude that every subspace must contain the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}.$ So, if a set does not contain $\\mathbf{0}$ then it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor instance, the set \n\n![](images/a1/a15e0b4fc4544421e572d15b8fd016c3fa8721e0ecf3f8808a469c3252d1f6d6.svg)\n\ndoes *not* contain the zero vector (since $0 \\neq 0 + 1$), and consequently it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186121126 1064 "A **subspace** of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^n$ that is closed  under vector addition and scalar multiplication. In other words: \n\n$H\\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nFor example, consider the line $L$ with equation $y=3x$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2?$ \n\nFirst, notice that we can express the points on $L$ as the following set:\n\n![](images/1f/1f380f9928da2dd44ff157a72fce97c02ae44626df515d52ece6ff5feee302d2.svg)\n\nIn other words, $L$ contains all vectors of the form ![](images/90/90996c2ffae29c3bd9cd3ab398cca4dd7e1b8fbe275de8520a72a16e504fb0f3.svg) where $x$ is a real number.\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R^2,$ we need to check the two conditions above. \n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, ![](images/59/59be22e80d0be32c84a8c2bdb2f2c96b23a0941d4aba26bdefa078fd299c137c.svg) and ![](images/d7/d78ea4fcfbd469e88b7fe46e28ac6f396a538c577f4defdd1b3d60e60df3c4b7.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =\\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{v}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}+3{v}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, ![](images/75/759c7883ad2772f20a090ad2f8c5dfe01031ca31e2b5f0e7681c945c9256c91d.svg) Then, for any scalar $c$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}c{u}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n**Note:** Since the second condition must be satisfied for any $c,$ including $c=0,$ we can conclude that every subspace must contain the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}.$ So, if a set does not contain $\\mathbf{0}$ then it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor instance, the set \n\n![](images/a1/a15e0b4fc4544421e572d15b8fd016c3fa8721e0ecf3f8808a469c3252d1f6d6.svg)\n\ndoes *not* contain the zero vector (since $0 \\neq 0 + 1$), and consequently it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186121127 1064 "Recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n*Closure under vector addition:* \n\n $\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n*Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n $\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nLet $L$ be the line in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,1,-2).$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$ \n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n![](images/25/25fa34994b33e0d11fd8990dddbfda276857e699018b4701c6e2eaf13acb0c20.svg)\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 