thbf{b}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R^T$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $(R^T)^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ \\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\,R\\phantom{|}}\\,\\,x & ={Q}^{T}\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are $R$ and $Q^T.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186133803 1038 17592186564531 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186133926 1038 17592186564488 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186134132 1068 17592186130948 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186134133 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186134133 1071 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x, y, z) = (y - x)i$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t) = (t^{2} - t)i + (t^{3} - t)j + \\sin (2πt)k$ for $t ∈ [0, 1)$." 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186134133 1071 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=(y-x)\\,i$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+({t}^{3}-t)\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186134133 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-t,\\,y={t}^{3}-t,\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(y-x)\\,i \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-t-{t}^{2}+t)\\,i \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})\\,i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-1)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\,({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})\\,i\\,)\\cdot (\\,(2t-1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-1)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k\\,) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})\\cdot (2t-1)+0\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1)+0\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t) \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}-{t}^{3}-2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}-3{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\,2{t}^{4}-3{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}\\,)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{t}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{3{t}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{3})-(0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186134378 1068 17592186132307 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186135143 1064 "Let's find the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ where $A = QR,$ and\n![](images/4e/4e2433312f3a424aa161d5aeec63df38787c43435a9c2af44ba6b3d63f9ef065.svg)\n\nPreviously, we showed that the normal equation corresponding to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where $A=QR,$ can be reduced to\n\n$$\nR \\mathbf{x} = Q^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our matrices into this equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4(2) & =2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =10 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ is ![](images/72/723835f5ecb612e7d79c29ada9cc05241ffd4f233e29c9ed9a643994580a8c36.svg)" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186135143 1064 "Let's find the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ where $A = QR,$ and\n![](images/4e/4e2433312f3a424aa161d5aeec63df38787c43435a9c2af44ba6b3d63f9ef065.svg)\n\nPreviously, we showed that the normal equation corresponding to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where $A=QR,$ can be reduced to\n\n$$\nR \\mathbf{x} = Q^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our matrices into this equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 3{x}_{2} &=6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4(2) & =2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =10 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ is ![](images/72/723835f5ecb612e7d79c29ada9cc05241ffd4f233e29c9ed9a643994580a8c36.svg)" 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186135928 1078 17592186135929 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186138064 1071 "What is the sum of the first and last terms of the following sequence?\n\n$3,\\quad-5,\\quad7,\\quad-9,\\quad11,\\quad-13,\\quad15$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 false] [17592186138064 1071 "What is the sum of the first and last terms of the following sequence? \n\n$$\n3,\\quad-5,\\quad7,\\quad-9,\\quad11,\\