61 1075 17592186564355 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564461 1076 17592186045493 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564461 1077 "selection" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564461 1078 17592186564316 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564461 1079 #uuid "f2d8f279-2cfe-5521-bbd6-ad8903d4bc62" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564462 1075 17592186564049 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564462 1075 17592186564136 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564462 1075 17592186564195 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564462 1075 17592186564203 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564462 1075 17592186564299 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564462 1076 17592186045493 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564462 1077 "selection" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564462 1078 17592186564136 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564462 1079 #uuid "f733173d-d399-586b-b3ed-e56eee05876e" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564463 1075 17592186564021 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564463 1075 17592186564227 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564463 1075 17592186564239 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564463 1075 17592186564244 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564463 1075 17592186564278 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564463 1076 17592186045493 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564463 1077 "selection" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564463 1078 17592186564021 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564463 1079 #uuid "fbec5fcc-3c3a-5ffd-b0f3-92a5d7992122" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564464 1068 17592186564442 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564464 1069 "q-128962" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564464 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564464 1071 "What is the slope of the line $4x-2y=15?$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564464 1072 #uuid "01d6b59b-be2b-52a2-b7cf-129c1864b658" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564464 1074 "We can find the slope of the line if we can express its equation in slope-intercept form. To do this, we solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2y & =15 \\\\ -2y & =-4x+15 \\\\ 2y & =4x-15 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{2} & =\\frac{4x-15}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{4x}{2}-\\frac{15}{2} \\\\ y & =2x-\\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the slope-intercept equation, we can tell that the slope of the line is $m=2.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564465 1068 17592186564429 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564465 1069 "q-263003" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564465 1070 17592186045486 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564465 1071 "If a sequence is defined as ${a}_{n}=7-3{n}^{2}$ for $n\\ge 1,$ then the sum of the third and fifth terms is{{field-1}}." 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564465 1072 #uuid "041652e3-e5d6-50e5-af62-e1885be6626d" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564465 1074 "We substitute $n=3$ and $n=5$ into the definition of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =7-3(3)^{2}=-20 \\\\ {a}_{5} & =7-3(5)^{2}=-68\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the third and fifth terms is\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}+{a}_{5}=(-20)+(-68)=-88.\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564466 1068 17592186564400 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564466 1069 "q-171160" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564466 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564466 1071 "The lowest common denominator of $\\frac{5}{7}$ and $\\frac{1}{2}$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564466 1072 #uuid "093f60b7-b595-5220-994a-63101bc04a42" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564466 1074 "To find the lowest common denominator of two fractions, we first find the prime factorization of both denominators. \n\nSince the denominators $7$ and $2$ are prime numbers, they are already represented by their respective prime factorizations. \n\nNow, we write down each prime factor, and we raise each factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $7$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is also $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common denominator is ${7}^{1}\\cdot {2}^{1}=14.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564467 1068 17592186564463 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564467 1069 "q-158561" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564467 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564467 1071 "Find the domain of the function $z=\\frac{\\sqrt{y-{x}^{2}}}{2x-y}.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564467 1072 #uuid "0f0c6fb0-68b0-537c-ab8d-954592bf8ad5" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564467 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y-{x}^{2}\\ge 0 \\\\ 2x-y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge {x}^{2} \\\\ y\\ne 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge {x}^{2},\\,y\\ne 2x\\}.\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564468 1068 17592186564399 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564468 1069 "q-79956" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564468 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564468 1071 "Find the differential $\\text{d}f$ for the function $f(x,y)=xy+{y}^{2}.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564468 1072 #uuid "0f3c5c64-c0f1-58d5-8582-f8f8e159dad5" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564468 1074 "We compute the differential using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y\\,\\text{d}x+(x+2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564469 1068 17592186564435 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564469 1069 "q-51917" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564469 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564469 1071 "A sequence is defined as $f(n)=3n-1$ for $n\\ge 1.$ What is the $5$th term of the sequence?" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564469 1072 #uuid "0f81e122-7862-59a0-9cd2-28031f67f42e" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564469 1074 "To find the $5$th term, we substitute $n=5$ into the definition of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =3n-1 \\\\ f(5) & =3(5)-1 \\\\ f(5) & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $5$th term is $14.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564470 1068 17592186564405 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564470 1069 "q-41247" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564470 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564470 1071 "Determine the domain of the function $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{7}⁡(2x-3)+2.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564470 1072 #uuid "1124f708-b647-5fc7-9069-27535f9fe6bb" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564470 1074 "The domain of $y={log}_{7}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\nx>0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x),$ we replace $x$ with $2x-3$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n2x-3>0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & >0 \\\\ 2x & >3 \\\\ x & >\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in (\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564471 1068 17592186564395 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564471 1069 "q-54447" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564471 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564471 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{log}_{3}⁡(-9x)=$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564471 1072 #uuid "13360da3-e828-58d3-a8ca-a868355e39a5" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564471 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{3}⁡(-9x).$\n\n![](images/41/41378635430091e24674e22dbc45105bedd7af55410ba6069057f7a37b2025fc.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{log}_{3}⁡(-9x)=\\infty \n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564472 1068 17592186564396 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564472 1069 "q-130834" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564472 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564472 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨1,0,2⟩$ and $b=⟨0,2,3⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=-2$ and $f(b)=5,$ then what is missing from the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $\\text{_______________}\\,.$ *" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564472 1072 #uuid "17eec8d8-70be-5d2a-8c5e-928ea708ee3b" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564472 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ 5-(-2) & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨0,2,3⟩-⟨1,0,2⟩) \\\\ 7 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,1⟩ \\\\ 7 & =-{f}_{x}(c)+2{f}_{y}(c)+{f}_{z}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that ${f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)-{f}_{z}(c)=-7.$ *" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564473 1068 17592186564392 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564473 1069 "q-73730" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564473 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564473 1071 "Find the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(8p,-q)$ and $(-2p,-9q).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564473 1072 #uuid "1a0af22d-5fcd-5a62-b138-4a5969bb9fc8" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564473 1074 "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between ${{(8p,-q)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(-2p,-9q)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{8p+(-2p)}{2},\\frac{-q+(-9q)}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{8p-2p}{2},\\frac{-q-9q}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{6p}{2},\\frac{-10q}{2}) \\\\  & =(3p,-5q).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564474 1068 17592186564389 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564474 1069 "q-67658" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564474 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564474 1071 "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}{log}_{2}⁡(x+2).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564474 1072 #uuid "1d09c144-84e6-5d74-866a-0f1eb2fa303b" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564474 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{2}⁡(x+2).$\n\n![](images/5c/5c4b062938cc4e3d64f11f9ad3bd8da9431581e9a1b0278a977a64c0d727c73d.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le -2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}{log}_{2}⁡(x+2)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>-2.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}{log}_{2}⁡(x+2)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}{log}_{2}⁡(x+2)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564475 1068 17592186564403 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564475 1069 "q-81997" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564475 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564475 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+7).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564475 1072 #uuid "1e7e97e3-f06a-5ac7-917d-a28a5b06bd45" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564475 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+7)=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+7) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+7)} & ={e}^{0} \\\\ 2x+7 & =1 \\\\ 2x & =-6 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=-3.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564476 1068 17592186564421 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564476 1069 "q-54514" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564476 1070 17592186045486 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564476 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{log}_{3}⁡(4-2x)=$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564476 1072 #uuid "20f5e3df-f068-575a-bd88-8cc274ff8c19" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564476 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{3}⁡(4-2x).$\n\n![](images/b1/b1d65d2df4e6a5a6b66e908ed8f776e2a1b8a4b24896506d3625e6569d9ea4e6.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\ge 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{log}_{3}⁡(4-2x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x<2.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{log}_{3}⁡(4-2x)=-\\infty .\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564477 1068 17592186564445 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564477 1069 "q-158199" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564477 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564477 1071 "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-y}}?$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564477 1072 #uuid "28327fac-a614-5756-91bd-3fcab54fcda2" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564477 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx-y>0\\,⟹\\,y<x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y<x\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $>$ sign, the line is not part of the domain. Therefore, we draw a dashed line $y=x$ and shade the area *below* the line.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/77/77a1a03d037ee571fb0dc14ec8370c8b01f521b5a54798117c0b131000676599.png)" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564478 1068 17592186564456 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564478 1069 "q-78102" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564478 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564478 1071 "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to -3}{log}_{7}⁡(x+3).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564478 1072 #uuid "29481c76-b7fa-575a-9ace-40d060b32844" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564478 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{7}⁡(x+3).$\n\n![](images/0b/0b45cd648830ad88c2f21865c1bfd704783a7b5d9ce3510646ae55e98d2d95ac.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le -3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}{log}_{7}⁡(x+3)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>-3.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}{log}_{7}⁡(x+3)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -3}{log}_{7}⁡(x+3)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564479 1068 17592186564431 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564479 1069 "q-102703" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564479 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564479 1071 "Given the function $f(x,y,z)=x{z}^{2}+z{y}^{3}$, find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes  through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,1,2).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564479 1072 #uuid "30043088-8017-56c1-83d2-93da6bb272cf" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564479 1074 "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(-2,1,2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{2}+z{y}^{3})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{z}^{2}+z{y}^{3})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{z}^{2}+z{y}^{3})\\text{ }k \\\\  & ={z}^{2}\\text{ }i+3z{y}^{2}\\text{ }j+(2xz+{y}^{3})k \\\\ ∇\\,f(-2,1,2) & ={2}^{2}\\text{ }i+3(2)(1)^{2}\\text{ }j+(2(-2)(2)+{1}^{3})k \\\\  & =4\\,i+6\\,j-7k \\\\  & =⟨4,6,-7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-2,1,2)\\cdot ⟨x+2,\\,y-1,\\,z-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨4,6,-7⟩\\cdot ⟨x+2,\\,y-1,\\,z-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ 4(x+2)+6(y-1)-7(z-2) & =0 \\\\ 4x+6y-7z+16 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564480 1068 17592186564382 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564480 1068 17592186564460 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564480 1069 "q-333610" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564480 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564480 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}-{t}^{2}+3t-2 \\\\ -4t+8\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-t+1 \\\\ 2t-3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564480 1072 #uuid "338f9068-6af3-5a99-af8d-48b1ac33890b" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564480 1074 "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-t+1 \\\\ 2t-3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{0} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2(0)}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-0+1 \\\\ 2(0)-3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+1 \\\\ {c}_{2}-3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+1=2 \\\\ {c}_{2}-3=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=1.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{2}=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-t+1 \\\\ 2t-3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564481 1068 17592186564444 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564481 1069 "q-75642" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564481 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564481 1071 "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564481 1072 #uuid "34925275-ae4c-5d65-95f3-737bbc060369" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564481 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=2{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}-2{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{a}_{1}+(-1){a}_{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=-1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564482 1068 17592186564411 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564482 1069 "q-166673" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564482 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564482 1071 "![](images/34/34054404a184f9480abec1692e343f7ffbae4664c9c906e744f07cc082b0ebde.png)\n\nWhat are the roots of the function $f(x)$ defined by the mapping diagram shown above?" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564482 1072 #uuid "3cdee882-c7e4-5094-92c4-9cde591d4ea6" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564482 1074 "The roots of the function are the values of $x$ such that $f(x)=0.$ In the given mapping diagram, we see that there is no value of $x$ that gives an output of zero. \n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has no roots." 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564483 1068 17592186564439 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564483 1069 "q-105286" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564483 1070 17592186045486 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564483 1071 "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=4x-6y-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564483 1072 #uuid "451d76ac-89a7-51b9-831d-a0c0d1695961" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564483 1074 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-6y-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-6y-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4-2x & -6-6y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4-2x=0 \\\\ -6-6y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n4-2x=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n-6-6y=0\\,⟹\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(2,-1)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-2x)=-2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-6-6y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-2x)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-6-6y)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(2,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(2,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(2,-1)=det({f}^{″}(2,-1))=(-2)\\cdot (-6)-{0}^{2}=12>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)=({f}^{″}(2,-1))_{11}=-2<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(2,-1).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564484 1068 17592186564422 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564484 1068 17592186564441 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564484 1069 "q-223474" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564484 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564484 1071 "A sequence is defined as $f(n)={n}^{2}-1$ for $n\\ge 1.$ What are the $3$rd and $6$th terms of the sequence?\n\nThe $3$rd term is\n{{field-1}}\nand the $6$th term is\n{{field-2}}." 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564484 1072 #uuid "4c253992-f8f6-54f1-85fd-c768c36b2399" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564484 1074 "Using the definition of the sequence, we find the $3$rd and $6$th terms corresponding to $n=3$ and $n=6,$ respectively:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & ={3}^{2}-1=8 \\\\ f(6) & ={6}^{2}-1=35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $3$rd term is $8$ and the $6$th term is $35.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564485 1068 17592186564414 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564485 1069 "q-156682" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564485 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564485 1071 "Let $L$ be the line with equation $x+y=1$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564485 1072 #uuid "4d8749ea-387f-52be-af2d-efee12373f36" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564485 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 1-x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $x+y=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (0)+(0) & =1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 1+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (1)+(1) & =1 \\\\ 2 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564486 1068 17592186564437 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564486 1069 "q-54470" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564486 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564486 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to 4}{log}_{2}⁡(4x)=$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564486 1072 #uuid "580b167f-860a-55e7-8509-7c52af963b20" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564486 1074 "The function ${log}_{2}⁡(4x)$ is well defined for $x>0,$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=4$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}{log}_{2}⁡(4x) & ={log}_{2}⁡(4\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(16) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{4}) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564487 1068 17592186564381 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564487 1069 "q-105317" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564487 1070 17592186045486 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564487 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -13\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nIII. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solution." 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564487 1072 #uuid "5873b1df-0474-5771-9ef7-dcd0b294d997" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564487 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+20{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-13{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 20 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -13 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -13 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(0)+9(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are false, while statement II is true." 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564488 1068 17592186564401 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564488 1069 "q-162231" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564488 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564488 1071 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=x\\,i-z\\,j+y\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=2\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564488 1072 #uuid "598960e7-33b5-506e-acd3-0cfad9c377b7" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564488 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i-z\\,j+y\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\pi \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\pi [t]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi (2-0) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564489 1068 17592186564416 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564489 1069 "q-88002" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564489 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564489 1071 "![](images/99/99212835e0eec95ac4ebd0c7d0fd49e1bb7e1a392f53251aa49510ac8ef5b65d.png)\n\nWhich of the above quadrilaterals are similar to $T$ (other than $T$ itself)?" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564489 1072 #uuid "5a648c7b-b62d-5742-8e25-e411a6e50067" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564489 1074 "First, notice that $T$ is a right trapezoid with one pair of parallel opposite sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given polygons.\n\n- $K$ is *not* similar to $T.$ The polygon $K$ is a right trapezoid, and the corresponding angles are congruent, but the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are not the same: \n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{14}\\ne \\frac{6}{6}\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* similar to $T$ because $R$ is not a trapezoid (it's a rectangle since it has $2$ pairs of opposite parallel sides, and $2$ pairs of right angles).\n\n- $M$ is similar to $T.$ Indeed, $M$ is a right trapezoid. Furthermore, the corresponding angles are congruent, and the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{14}=\\frac{3}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$M$ only\"." 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564490 1068 17592186564427 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564490 1069 "q-105765" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564490 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564490 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨2,-1,3⟩$ and $b=⟨3,-1,1⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=6$ and $f(b)=2,$ then what is missing from the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $\\text{_______________}\\,.$ *" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564490 1072 #uuid "602e236f-f7ee-52d9-b1ca-e9d90f7ae370" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564490 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ 2-6 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨3,-1,1⟩-⟨2,-1,3⟩) \\\\ -4 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\\\ -4 & ={f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{z}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that ${f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{z}(c)=-4.$ *" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564491 1068 17592186564457 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564491 1069 "q-158686" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564491 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564491 1071 "![](images/e0/e006f41f3c8a0201623c88bd4f3738bea05258690c8a0adef297769341a3f490.png)\n\nGiven that $r$, $s$ and $t$ in the above diagram are parallel, find $m∠1.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564491 1072 #uuid "629276db-5577-5de0-b4ed-4cb541d19524" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564491 1074 "Consider $∠2$ in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/2b/2bca55a68d4a0695c73f82ca82d5fae386d9b8d57b058bc1cf694bee902cff25.png)\n\nNotice that $∠2$ and the angle whose measure is ${42}^{∘}$ are alternate exterior angles formed when the transversal $u$ cuts the parallel lines $r$ and $s.$ Hence, \n\n$$\nm∠2={42}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that the angle determined by $∠1$ and $∠2$ and the angle whose measure is ${108}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed when the transversal $v$ cuts the parallel lines $s$ and $t.$ Hence, \n\n$$\nm∠1+m∠2={108}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $m∠2$ into the equation, we can find $m∠1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1+m∠2 & ={108}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1+{42}^{∘} & ={108}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1 & ={66}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564492 1068 17592186564449 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564492 1069 "q-106068" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564492 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564492 1071 "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨3,7⟩,$ respectively." 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564492 1072 #uuid "6547ba2d-be83-5b17-a5ce-a14f2d6a7139" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564492 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨3,7⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,7)-f(0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨3,7⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\ ({7}^{2}-{3}^{2})-({1}^{2}-{0}^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,6⟩ \\\\ 39 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-2x,2y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-2{c}_{1},2{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}39 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨3,6⟩ \\\\ 39 & =⟨-2{c}_{1},2{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,6⟩ \\\\ 39 & =-6{c}_{1}+12{c}_{2} \\\\ 13 & =-2{c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{7-1} & =\\frac{x-0}{3-0} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{6} & =\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y-1 & =2x \\\\ y & =2x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =2{c}_{1}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $-2{c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}=13,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}+4(2{c}_{1}+1) & =13 \\\\ -2{c}_{1}+8{c}_{1}+4 & =13 \\\\ 6{c}_{1} & =9 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{2}=2{c}_{1}+1=4.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{3}{2},4).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564493 1068 17592186564461 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564493 1069 "q-156650" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564493 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564493 1071 "Let $L$ be the line with equation $3x+y=4$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564493 1072 #uuid "659cad17-10ef-5a6e-af0d-5c68299c5ad8" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564493 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ -3x+4\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $y=-3x+4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+4 \\\\ (0) & =-3(0)+4 \\\\ 0 & =4\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example,$a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 4+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+4 \\\\ (5) & =-3(1)+4 \\\\ 5 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564494 1068 17592186564419 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564494 1069 "q-101290" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564494 1070 17592186045486 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564494 1071 "A sequence is defined as ${a}_{n}=2n+5$ for $n\\ge 1.$ What is the sum of the third and fourth terms?" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564494 1072 #uuid "69da1904-3ed5-5a22-8299-a97a8db45c15" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564494 1074 "We substitute $n=3$ and $n=4$ into the definition of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =2(3)+5=11 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =2(4)+5=13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the third and fourth terms is\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}+{a}_{4}=11+13=24.\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564495 1068 17592186564430 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564495 1069 "q-81042" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564495 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564495 1071 "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & \\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564495 1072 #uuid "74b82340-7a7b-5e95-8461-8f632c1a4fe8" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564495 1074 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & \\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\n \nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{1}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ is an stationary point of $f(x,y).$  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y})=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y})=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=det({f}^{″}(0,1))={0}^{2}-1\\cdot 1-=-1<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,1)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,1).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564496 1068 17592186564378 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564496 1069 "q-117944" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564496 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564496 1071 "![](images/f2/f2dc2ed0562e3d93888ab025195ada5da5ef9bf5312d6215dfb83253c81f9be7.png)\n\nOrder the sides of $△KLM$ above from the shortest to the longest." 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564496 1072 #uuid "78772149-6410-5606-9ad3-23c6e01f9b0e" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564496 1074 "Since $∠K$ is the smallest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{LM}$ is the shortest side of the triangle.\n\nSince $∠M$ is the largest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{KL}$ must be the longest side of the triangle. \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nLM<KM<KL\n$$\n\nand therefore the required order is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{LM},\\,\\overset{―}{KM},\\,\\overset{―}{KL}.\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564497 1068 17592186564424 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564497 1069 "q-51910" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564497 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564497 1071 "What is the sum of the fifth, sixth, and seventh terms of the following sequence?\n\n$$\n-9,\\,-6,\\,-3,\\,0,\\,6,\\,12,\\,18\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564497 1072 #uuid "7a74b26f-0105-5ef0-8131-fbfddb81968a" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564497 1074 "For this sequence:\n\n- The first term is $-9$\n\n- The second term is $-6$\n\n- The third term is $-3$\n\n- The fourth term is $0$\n\n- The fifth term is $6$\n\n- The sixth term is $12$\n\n- The seventh term is $18$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the fifth, sixth, and seventh terms is \n\n$$\n6+12+18=36.\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564498 1068 17592186564451 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564498 1069 "q-158186" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564498 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564498 1071 "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡y}{{x}^{3}}.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564498 1072 #uuid "7a819c67-d4ea-5946-94af-b50fc8d8ad96" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564498 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y>0 \\\\ {x}^{3}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}y>0 \\\\ x\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>0,\\,x\\ne 0\\}.\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564499 1068 17592186564436 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564499 1069 "q-97069" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564499 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564499 1071 "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564499 1072 #uuid "7d1c2efa-cbed-55e3-967d-daf6bf46e742" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564499 1074 "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the points $(2,-6)$ and $(-1,3),$ which are defined by the columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{3-(-6)}{-1-2}=-3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=-3x.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564500 1068 17592186564402 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564500 1069 "q-81862" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564500 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564500 1071 "Determine the range of the function $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡(x+4)-3.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564500 1072 #uuid "800d4066-ddae-513f-a0b6-998ce2ab2521" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564500 1074 "The range of the function $y={log}_{2}⁡(x)$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nHorizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564501 1068 17592186564407 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564501 1068 17592186564450 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564501 1068 17592186564453 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564501 1069 "q-333014" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564501 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564501 1071 "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} & -\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}-6{t}^{2}+\\frac{41}{3}t-8 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-\\frac{13}{3}t+5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n    $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n    $\\,\\,{t}^{2}+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n    $\\,\\,t+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n    {{field-2}}\n  </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n    .\n  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n    $-2$\n  </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564501 1072 #uuid "83030669-b615-545c-b0dd-a292bd26151d" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564501 1074 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-6{t}^{2}+\\frac{41}{3}t-8 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-\\frac{13}{3}t+5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]{t}^{2}+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} & -\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-6{t}^{2}+\\frac{41}{3}t-8 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-\\frac{13}{3}t+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{5{a}_{1}-8{a}_{2}}{3}){t}^{2}+(\\frac{5{b}_{1}-8{b}_{2}}{3})t+(\\frac{5{c}_{1}-8{c}_{2}}{3}) \\\\ (\\frac{-4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}}{3}){t}^{2}+(\\frac{-4{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}}{3})t+(\\frac{-4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}}{3})\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-6{t}^{2}+\\frac{41}{3}t-8 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-\\frac{13}{3}t+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0{t}^{2}+2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 0{t}^{2}+2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{5{a}_{1}-8{a}_{2}-18}{3}){t}^{2}+(\\frac{5{b}_{1}-8{b}_{2}+41}{3})t+(\\frac{5{c}_{1}-8{c}_{2}-24}{3}) \\\\ (\\frac{-4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+9}{3}){t}^{2}+(\\frac{-4{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}-13}{3})t+(\\frac{-4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+15}{3})\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coefficients of like powers of $t,$ we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{a}_{1}-8{a}_{2}-18}{3}=0 \\\\ \\frac{-4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+9}{3}=0 \\\\ \\frac{5{b}_{1}-8{b}_{2}+41}{3}=2{a}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{-4{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}-13}{3}=2{a}_{2} \\\\ \\frac{5{c}_{1}-8{c}_{2}-24}{3}={b}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{-4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+15}{3}={b}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first and second equations, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=2,\\,{a}_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the second and third equations, and solving for ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=-1,\\,{b}_{2}=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these last two values into the fourth and sixth equations, and solving for ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=1,\\,{c}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}-t+1 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+3t-2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{t}^{2}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564502 1068 17592186564452 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564502 1069 "q-157254" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564502 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564502 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $3{a}_{1}-2{a}_{2}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent." 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564502 1072 #uuid "848dcb43-d675-54a9-ae47-a5c4f5dfb881" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564502 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-2{a}_{2} & =3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -21\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564503 1068 17592186564386 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564503 1069 "q-53664" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564503 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564503 1071 "![](images/01/01b6ade0a9b4945c0381848092e9b2289f823c49b2c5c138a29187e7f541787c.png)\n\nThe two pentagons shown above are similar. What is the correct similarity statement?" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564503 1072 #uuid "889c45f8-a956-5ef6-ac2b-6cd191ad30dc" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564503 1074 "Since, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠A≅∠U,\\,∠B≅∠V,\\,∠C≅∠W,\\,∠D≅∠X,\\,∠E≅∠Y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n- $A$ corresponds to $U,$\n\n- $B$ corresponds to $V,$\n\n- $C$ corresponds to $W,$\n\n- $D$ corresponds to $X,$\n\n- $E$ corresponds to $Y.$\n\nTherefore, $ABCDE∼UVWXY.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564504 1068 17592186564390 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564504 1069 "q-158183" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564504 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564504 1071 "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)}.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564504 1072 #uuid "8a09ec73-a8f5-5e32-98fd-165c3e60bda6" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564504 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y>0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y>0 \\\\ x-y\\ne 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x-y>0,\\,x-y\\ne 1\\}.\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564505 1068 17592186564420 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564505 1069 "q-158549" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564505 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564505 1071 "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}-1}.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564505 1072 #uuid "8e55ad1f-d84a-5ded-9fca-459293bc995a" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564505 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-1\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}\\ne 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\ne 1\\}.\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564506 1068 17592186564383 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564506 1069 "q-37587" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564506 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564506 1071 "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})<0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})>0$\n\nIII. The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is a local maximum of $f$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564506 1072 #uuid "90239ba3-7e56-5134-995c-b697d9dca4dc" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564506 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we obtain   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})=det({f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=(-1)^{2}-{0}^{2}=1>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})={({f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}))}_{11}=-1<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})<0,$ the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564507 1068 17592186564438 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564507 1069 "q-106066" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564507 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564507 1071 "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)=xy$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4⟩,$ respectively." 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564507 1072 #uuid "90802828-69ad-5b22-b55e-e0d3346dd2ca" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564507 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=xy$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,4)-f(0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,4⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\ 2(4)-0(0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4⟩ \\\\ 8 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨y,x⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨{c}_{2},{c}_{1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4⟩ \\\\ 8 & =⟨{c}_{2},{c}_{1}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4⟩ \\\\ 8 & =2{c}_{2}+4{c}_{1} \\\\ 4 & ={c}_{2}+2{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-0}{4-0} & =\\frac{x-0}{2-0} \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =2{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $2{c}_{2}+4{c}_{1}=8,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+2{c}_{1} & =4 \\\\ 4{c}_{1} & =4 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{2}=2{c}_{1}=2.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(1,2).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564508 1068 17592186564387 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564508 1069 "q-158571" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564508 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564508 1071 "Evaluate $z=\\frac{2y-x}{{x}^{3}-{y}^{2}-4}$ at the point $(2,-2).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564508 1072 #uuid "911398b5-8165-51fb-be3f-bfee0bb6f77f" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564508 1074 "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(2,-2),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=-2$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{|}_{(2,-2)} & =\\frac{2y-x}{{x}^{3}-{y}^{2}-4}\\,{|}_{(2,-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-2)-2}{{2}^{3}-(-2)^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4-2}{8-4-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-6)}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined." 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564509 1068 17592186564415 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564509 1069 "q-67653" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564509 1070 17592186045486 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564509 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6-2x)=$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564509 1072 #uuid "94ed6f2f-e6d7-5eca-a61d-41902b4a3cb4" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564509 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6-2x).$\n\n![](images/bb/bba697be09903e54cffc4bf0c0e05cbd30cbfc11975cac5097e964415baca113.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x>3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6-2x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x<3.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6-2x)=-\\infty .\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564510 1068 17592186564413 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564510 1069 "q-118706" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564510 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564510 1071 "![](images/1b/1b3636b4a0534a8794c7b7955aa16d8bb90d98dcdaebbc40311c2e13e971e7af.png)\n\nGiven $△PQR$ shown above, which of the following diagrams represents the height corresponding to $\\overset{―}{PR}?$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564510 1072 #uuid "97cef597-7bfc-539c-89dd-d586cabec0a9" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564510 1074 "The height (or altitude) corresponding to $\\overset{―}{PR}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $Q$ to the line $\\overset{⟷}{PR},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{⟷}{PR}.$\n\nTo draw the height, we proceed as follows:\n\n- Extend the line segment $\\overset{―}{PR}.$\n\n![](images/94/94e65bdbb5e82c72b97f69ab2da1b16e2efe8399a83e78a2721d1e74eaa9b8b4.png)\n\n- Draw the perpendicular from $Q$ to $\\overset{⟷}{PR}.$\n\n![](images/aa/aa3254d24cd0d21a8a78009c8c07591e34ab46fa0449fc27e179d5a32ac70a17.png)" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564511 1068 17592186564418 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564511 1069 "q-158585" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564511 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564511 1071 "Evaluate $z={2}^{-x}+\\frac{1}{y}$ at the point $(2,2).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564511 1072 #uuid "9c34765f-7311-53ab-be9b-191b42487450" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564511 1074 "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(2,2),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=2$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{|}_{(2,2)} & =({2}^{-x}+\\frac{1}{y}){|}_{(2,2)} \\\\  & ={2}^{-2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564512 1068 17592186564443 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564512 1069 "q-158554" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564512 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564512 1071 "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564512 1072 #uuid "a1824679-625d-5463-b2f3-fb72a493af82" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564512 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\}.\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564513 1068 17592186564459 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564513 1069 "q-5926" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564513 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564513 1071 "![](images/c3/c3a31aad745cafe59f0c15e56dd48bf9350e46c08e148b843a6904145934e46b.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ given the figure above." 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564513 1072 #uuid "a1a77f56-694e-539a-a24b-49a13b3865ca" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564513 1074 "Since the two angles are supplementary, the sum of their measures is equal to ${180}^{∘}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{48}^{∘}+(x+{42}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x+{90}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564514 1068 17592186564404 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564514 1069 "q-105745" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564514 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564514 1071 "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨2,5⟩$ and $b=⟨3,-2⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=-3$ and $f(b)=5,$ then according to the mean value theorem, which of the following must be true for some point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c?$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564514 1072 #uuid "a261cf1f-d1e0-58d8-b832-e9ddda3c5b83" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564514 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)·(b-a) \\\\ 5-(-3) & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨3,-2⟩-⟨2,5⟩) \\\\ 8 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-7⟩ \\\\ 8 & ={f}_{x}(c)-7{f}_{y}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564515 1068 17592186564408 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564515 1069 "q-102702" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564515 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564515 1071 "Given the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+{z}^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes  through the point where $(x,y,z)=(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4,1).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564515 1072 #uuid "a6978155-572b-505c-b00f-8a94900d562b" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564515 1074 "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+{z}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-2{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\text{ }i+{e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\text{ }j+2zk \\\\ ∇\\,f(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4,1) & =-2{e}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\text{ }i+{e}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\text{ }j+2(1)k \\\\  & =-2{e}^{4}\\,i+0\\,j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-2{e}^{4},0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4,1)\\cdot ⟨x-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,y-4,\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-2{e}^{4},0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,y-4,\\,z-1⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564516 1068 17592186564406 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564516 1069 "q-118707" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564516 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564516 1071 "![](images/81/812bde152b84abaf518f6e9b09c43824786c724eac768d83e4d53615d69e1693.png)\n\nGiven $△PQR$ shown above, which of the following diagrams represents the height corresponding to $\\overset{―}{PQ}?$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564516 1072 #uuid "a800f321-defb-572d-a55b-71be8add2663" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564516 1074 "The height (or altitude) corresponding to $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $R$ to the line $\\overset{⟷}{PQ},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{⟷}{PQ}.$\n\nTo draw the height, we proceed as follows:\n\n- Extend the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}.$\n\n![](images/26/265ee40283911b754264224b3b0f349b24cae377373941304865b336a53c7b8e.png)\n\n- Draw the perpendicular from $R$ to $\\overset{⟷}{PQ}.$\n\n![](images/2e/2e48106e4aedb44faef00060f6af9632bd0c8e62eb8d06f12606c2a476bacac3.png)" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564517 1068 17592186564447 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564517 1069 "q-158194" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564517 1070 17592186045486 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564517 1071 "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2})?$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564517 1072 #uuid "aca8e51f-98b0-5cbe-9d48-2cf6165eb100" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564517 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n4-{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<4\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}<1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}<1\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $<$ sign, the curve is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the dashed curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}<1$ and shade the region *inside* the curve.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/59/59ac983361be7dd9a991e1cf134e7193256891a5587eab6bff5edf906fef3688.png)" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564518 1068 17592186564454 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564518 1069 "q-111439" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564518 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564518 1071 "Find all of the real solutions to the equation $(x-3)^{4}=-16.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564518 1072 #uuid "b024a1aa-681f-5f33-860b-bc65702c1e19" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564518 1074 "Notice that $(x-3)^{4}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x$ because the exponent ($4$) is even. This expression can never be negative. \n\nTherefore, there are no real solutions to this equation." 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564519 1068 17592186564428 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564519 1069 "q-157293" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564519 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564519 1071 "Let $L$ be the line with equation $2x-y=3$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564519 1072 #uuid "b0506b4c-4bb7-5b50-9f2e-88bb9d3c3ed6" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564519 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2x-3\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $2x-y=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =3 \\\\ 2(0)-(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ -3-1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =3 \\\\ 2(1)-(-4) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564520 1068 17592186564380 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564520 1069 "q-158568" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564520 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564520 1071 "The function $f(x,y)$ is defined as \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(y+1).\n$$\n\nEvaluate this function at the point $(-3,1).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564520 1072 #uuid "be7cd3c6-b4d8-58fc-a0bb-f9185ad55778" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564520 1074 "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(-3,1),$ we substitute $x=-3$ and $y=1$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3,1) & =x\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(y+1)\\,{|}_{(-3,1)} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1+1) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{arccos}⁡2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined." 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564521 1068 17592186564384 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564521 1069 "q-78092" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564521 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564521 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{log}_{5}⁡(x+1)=$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564521 1072 #uuid "c386627f-74f0-5d5a-9a11-2fd0a0613fa9" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564521 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+1).$\n\n![](images/90/902c8394181aefb301c901b3792e89e909ba36ecba3a9b8113fc42b80fd08870.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{log}_{5}⁡(x+1)=\\infty \n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564522 1068 17592186564410 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564522 1069 "q-158688" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564522 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564522 1071 "![](images/6f/6f1cb5dc40aec6a6a6402d01c50ed85d30c7992606d9957b92084a0a097823c4.png)\n\nGiven that $r$, $s$ and $t$ in the above diagram are parallel, find $m∠1.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564522 1072 #uuid "cc803095-076a-5b93-b2c7-da97793184ac" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564522 1074 "Consider $∠2$ in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/5a/5afb2a0f9f26dc3c02b52ac50743e9541f587349267b0c322cb2644b09c48617.png)\n\nNotice that $∠2$ and the angle whose measure is ${66}^{∘}$ are alternate exterior angles formed when the transversal $v$ cuts the parallel lines $r$ and $s.$ Hence, \n\n$$\nm∠2={66}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that the angle determined by $∠1$ and $∠2$ and the angle whose measure is ${127}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed when the transversal $u$ cuts the parallel lines $s$ and $t.$ Hence, \n\n$$\nm∠1+m∠2={127}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $m∠2$ into the equation, we can find $m∠1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1+m∠2 & ={127}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1+{66}^{∘} & ={127}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1 & ={61}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564523 1068 17592186564398 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564523 1069 "q-166551" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564523 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564523 1071 "Find the maximum value of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x-4)+3.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564523 1072 #uuid "cf10c890-1bed-5075-9d7f-bb216357cf94" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564523 1074 "The range of the function $y=\\operatorname{log}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nHorizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ and the function has no maximum value." 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564524 1068 17592186564448 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564524 1068 17592186564455 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564524 1069 "q-262870" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564524 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564524 1071 "What are the second and last terms of the following sequence? \n\n$$\n7,\\,14,\\,21,\\,28,\\,35,\\,42\n$$\n\nThe second term is\n{{field-1}}\nand the last term is\n{{field-2}}." 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564524 1072 #uuid "d2d2cf77-d5f7-5477-9cd1-8f7ee0800051" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564524 1074 "For this sequence:\n\n- The first term is $7$\n\n- The second term is $14$\n\n- The third term is $21$\n\n- The fourth term is $28$\n\n- The fifth term is $35$\n\n- The sixth term is $42$\n\nTherefore, the second term is $14$ and the last term is $42.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564525 1068 17592186564397 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564525 1069 "q-339829" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564525 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564525 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+8)}{2-x}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564525 1072 #uuid "d5bc96f7-1e4b-5204-9506-eaed8cba8e20" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564525 1074 "The function $\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+8)}{2-x}$ is well defined for $x>-4,$ and $x\\ne 2$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=1$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+8)}{2-x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2(1)+8)}{2-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(10)}{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{1}) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564526 1068 17592186564434 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564526 1069 "q-60981" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564526 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564526 1071 "If $\\,\\boxed{}\\times 40%=32,$ then $\\,\\boxed{}=$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564526 1072 #uuid "d64cbb84-4920-5e00-a5fb-dd325fcf710c" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564526 1074 "The part is $32,$ the percentage is $40%,$ and we need to find the whole.\n\nTo do this, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{whole}=\\text{part}÷\\text{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $40%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{40}{100}=\\frac{40÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =32÷40% \\\\  & =32÷\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =32\\times \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{2}\\times 5 \\\\  & =16\\times 5 \\\\  & =80\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564527 1068 17592186564388 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564527 1069 "q-104974" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564527 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564527 1071 "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(2,-1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(2,-1)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)>0$\n\nIII. The point $(2,-1)$ is a local minimum of $f$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564527 1072 #uuid "d69aae31-136f-59cf-90af-d2507ee77b3a" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564527 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(2,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(2,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(2,-1)=det({f}^{″}(2,-1))=(-4)\\cdot (-6)-{0}^{2}=24>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)=({f}^{″}(2,-1))_{11}=-4<0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(2,-1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)<0,$ the point $(2,-1)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564528 1068 17592186564432 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564528 1069 "q-45552" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564528 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564528 1071 "A particular nature reserve started with $100$ rabbits. The number of rabbits is expected to increase sixfold each decade. What equation should we solve to determine the number of decades it will take for the rabbit population to increase to $129\\,600?$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564528 1072 #uuid "d77788ff-b482-51d4-bdb1-79e3cff6db85" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564528 1074 "We know that the number of rabbits increases sixfold every decade. So the number of rabbits, which we'll call $y(t),$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $6.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=100$ is the initial number of rabbits.\n\n- $b=6$ is the growth factor.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in decades, since the opening of the nature reserve.\n\nTherefore, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=100\\cdot {6}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSo, to find the time it takes for the population to increase to $129\\,600,$ we would need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =129\\,600 \\\\ 100\\cdot {6}^{t} & =129\\,600.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564529 1068 17592186564440 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564529 1069 "q-75636" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564529 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564529 1071 "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564529 1072 #uuid "de9e1bb6-8c29-536b-b329-05255b6d1f04" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564529 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]=-3[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=-3{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-3{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564530 1068 17592186564393 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564530 1069 "q-115562" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564530 1070 17592186045486 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564530 1071 "If $y$ varies inversely with $x$ and $y=2$ when $x=4,$ find the value of $y$ when $x=\\frac{4}{5}.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564530 1072 #uuid "dfc98e96-f028-5cd6-8e89-3ae59a19563f" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564530 1074 "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $y=2$ when $x=4.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{k}{4} \\\\ 2\\cdot 4 & =k \\\\ 8 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the inverse variation is given by \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{8}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the value of $x=\\frac{4}{5}$ into this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{8}{(\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =8÷\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{4} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564531 1068 17592186564425 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564531 1069 "q-139798" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564531 1070 17592186045486 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564531 1071 "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}8 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,R=\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564531 1072 #uuid "e0061631-6269-58a0-a054-4e33a330ba6f" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564531 1074 "The least-squares solution $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=-9 \\\\ {x}_{2}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=9.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8(9) & =-9 \\\\ 9{x}_{1} & =-81 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564532 1068 17592186564409 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564532 1068 17592186564426 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564532 1069 "q-333596" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564532 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564532 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564532 1072 #uuid "e4dc2c43-c8eb-5bcf-a39b-abeb3bbb2764" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564532 1074 "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0\\cdot {e}^{2(0)} \\\\ {e}^{2(0)}\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-0}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}+1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=3 \\\\ {c}_{2}+1=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we have ${c}_{1}=3.$ From the second equation, we get ${c}_{2}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564533 1068 17592186564379 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564533 1069 "q-158185" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564533 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564533 1071 "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)}{{e}^{x}}.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564533 1072 #uuid "e5fbae1b-fa0a-5638-bec4-5b64d04c9a07" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564533 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nNotice that ${e}^{x}>0$ for all $x.$ Therefore, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y>0\\,⟹\\,x>y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x>y\\}.\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564534 1068 17592186564458 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564534 1069 "q-158191" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564534 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564534 1071 "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2})}{y}.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564534 1072 #uuid "e70a4727-f486-57b6-aa40-dbf0dba8fb16" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564534 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}>0 \\\\ y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-1<x<1 \\\\ y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1<x<1,\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564535 1068 17592186564391 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564535 1069 "q-215145" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564535 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564535 1071 "Solve for $y$ where $-\\frac{1}{2y+7}=\\frac{4}{5y-2}.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564535 1072 #uuid "e8061560-b60f-50e8-a1e6-e8fcd0b1f879" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564535 1074 "First, note that $y=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $y=\\frac{2}{5}$ cannot be solutions since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction and then apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2y+7} & =\\frac{4}{5y-2} \\\\ \\frac{-1}{2y+7} & =\\frac{4}{5y-2} \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (5y-2) & =(2y+7)\\cdot 4 \\\\ -5y+2 & =8y+28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5y+2 & =8y+28 \\\\ -5y+2+5y & =8y+28+5y \\\\ 2 & =13y+28 \\\\ 2-28 & =13y+28-28 \\\\ -26 & =13y \\\\ \\frac{-26}{13} & =\\frac{13y}{13} \\\\ -2 & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y=-2$ is different from $y=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $y=\\frac{2}{5},$ we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $y=-2.$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564536 1068 17592186564394 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564536 1069 "q-61145" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564536 1070 17592186045486 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564536 1071 "A sequence is defined as ${a}_{n}=3n-2$ for $n\\ge 1.$ What is the sum of the third and fourth terms?" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564536 1072 #uuid "f29afdcb-efb1-549b-8468-0f53b64a94c5" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564536 1074 "We substitute $n=3$ and $n=4$ into the definition of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =3(3)-2=7 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =3(4)-2=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the third and fourth terms is\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}+{a}_{4}=7+10=17.\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564537 1068 17592186564417 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564537 1069 "q-73720" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564537 1070 17592186045485 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564537 1071 "Find the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(-w,7z)$ and $(5w,-3z).$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564537 1072 #uuid "f548e29c-4fb0-5de0-8aa0-2659faa5a2eb" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564537 1074 "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between ${{(-w,7z)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(5w,-3z)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-w+5w}{2},\\frac{7z+(-3z)}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-w+5w}{2},\\frac{7z-3z}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4w}{2},\\frac{4z}{2}) \\\\  & =(2w,2z).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564538 1068 17592186564433 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564538 1069 "q-158196" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564538 1070 17592186045487 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564538 1071 "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}?$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564538 1072 #uuid "f90f5dc8-4e9d-5e87-800a-708d8b32cd8d" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564538 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\ge 0\n$$\n\nSince the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\ge 0$ is true for all values of $x$ and $y,$ the domain of the function $f$ is the entire $xy$-plane.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/40/402d90507403eabf4cd35cde83a45c9fa2d8080347e1debb0e2463be408b489b.png)" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564539 1068 17592186564462 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564539 1069 "q-101278" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564539 1070 17592186045486 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564539 1071 "Which of the following statements is true regarding the sequence ${a}_{n}=4n+1$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\nI. Its first term is $5$\n\nII. Its first term is twice the second term\n\nIII. Its last term is $41$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564539 1072 #uuid "fc6510bd-b576-581a-8664-e81e95aec6a7" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564539 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- The first term of the sequence is represented by ${a}_{1}.$ Using the rule ${a}_{n}=4n+1,$ we compute ${a}_{1}=4+1=5.$ Therefore, statement I is true.\n\n- The second term of the sequence is represented by ${a}_{2}.$ Using the rule ${a}_{n}=4n+1,$ we compute ${a}_{2}=8+1=9.$  The first term was ${a}_{1}=5,$ and the second term is ${a}_{2}=9,$ so the first term is not twice the second term. Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- The sequence is defined for $n\\ge 1,$ which means that there is no limit to how large $n$ can be. There is no last term because the sequence keeps going on forever. Therefore, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564540 1058 17592186564577 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564540 1062 #uuid "09fb06eb-701d-5463-91d5-97d3fc405eb0" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564540 1063 17592186564491 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564541 1058 17592186564549 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564541 1062 #uuid "0d02eb78-b31f-5714-9473-a758d6d95bda" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564541 1063 17592186262636 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564542 1058 17592186564597 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186564542 1062 #uuid "0d37e1d3-6c33-5f0f-bc28-e7b63d04fffd" 1319413